{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And no meat of that which you slaughter on the evening of the first day shall remain until morning.</b> A warning against leaving over in the Passover of all generations, since it was stated only regarding the Passover of Egypt. And 'the first day' stated here is the fourteenth of Nisan, as you say: ״אַךְ בַּיּוֹם הָרִאשׁוֹן תַּשְׁבִּיתוּ שְׂאֹר מִבָּתֵּיכֶם״ (Exodus 12). And since the verse departed from the subject of Passover and began to speak in the laws of the seven days — such as 'seven days you shall eat matzot on it' and 'no leaven shall be seen in all your borders' — it needed to specify for which slaughter it is warning. For if it had written 'and no meat of that which you slaughter in the evening shall remain until morning,' I would say it is the shelamim slaughtered all seven days, under the prohibition of leaving over, which are eaten only for a day and a night — therefore it wrote <b>on the evening of the first day.</b> Another interpretation: the verse speaks of the festival offering of the fourteenth, and teaches about it that it is eaten for two days. And 'the first' stated here — the verse speaks of the first festival day. And so the sense of the verse is: meat of the festival offering that you slaughter in the evening shall not remain on the first festival day until the morning of the second — but it is eaten on the fourteenth and on the fifteenth. And so it is taught in tractate Pesaḥim (70a)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:47:20.624555298Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}